import type { StateNote } from './state-notes';
export const NOTES_A: Record<string, StateNote> = {
  AL: {
    intro: `Work out what an Alabama juror is owed, check whether your employer must keep paying you, and test an excuse against Alabama law before your report date.`,
    resume: `Alabama treats jury duty as a paid workday for full-time employees: under Ala. Code §§ 12-16-8 and 12-16-8.1 they keep their usual compensation, and an employer cannot force them to burn vacation, sick or unpaid leave. The court's own daily expense allowance and mileage are left by Ala. Code § 12-19-210 to an order or rule of the Alabama Supreme Court, and we could not confirm the amounts on a reachable official page, so the summons is the place to check. Show the summons to your immediate supervisor on the next workday. People 80 or older may ask in writing to be permanently exempt (§ 12-16-62). A juror chosen for a trial serves until it ends unless the judge excuses them, and after petit jury service you cannot be summoned again for 24 months. One postponement is automatic on request, to a date within 6 months. Ignoring a summons without good excuse is contempt: up to $300 and 10 days in county jail (§ 12-16-82).`,
    body: `<h2>Alabama employers and the full-time pay rule</h2><p>Alabama is one of the few states where the wage question is settled in the employee's favor by statute. A full-time employee summoned for jury duty is entitled to his or her usual compensation, and the absence is excused: the employer cannot require you to use vacation days, sick time or unpaid leave to cover it. The protection comes with a duty of your own. You must show the summons to your immediate supervisor on the next workday after you receive it, so keep a copy and hand it over promptly. The usual-pay sentence of § 12-16-8 speaks of full-time employees only, so part-time workers should ask about their employer's policy.</p><h2>Postponing or being excused in Alabama</h2><p>Section 12-16-63.1 gives everyone the right to postpone the first appearance once, by asking in person, by phone, by email or in writing; the new date must fall within 6 months. A second postponement is possible only for an extreme emergency. Separate from postponement, the court can excuse you for undue or extreme physical or financial hardship, an incapacitating mental or physical condition, being a nursing mother when the summons arrives, public necessity, or being a sole caregiver with no substitute. Missing work by itself is not a hardship, and an excused juror is then exempt for 24 months.</p>`,
  },
  AK: {
    intro: `Use this Alaska page to see what the statutes say about employer wages, deferral and exemptions, and to model juror pay once the court confirms its rate.`,
    resume: `Alaska's juror fees are fixed by the Alaska Court System under Administrative Rule 14, not by statute, and that court website could not be reached on October 29, 2026, so every pay figure here is unconfirmed; use the rate printed on your summons. The statutes themselves are clear on the rest. Alaska Stat. § 09.20.037 bars an employer from firing, threatening or penalizing you for answering a summons, yet it does not require wages for the days you serve. No age exemption exists: § 09.20.030 lets a person claim exemption for their own health, the health or care of family, a permanent disability, or a substantial hardship expected to last more than two years. Nobody can be made to serve more than three months in a two-year period, and anyone who served within the past year is disqualified. Deferral is possible to an agreed date no more than 10 months out. Neglecting a summons is contempt; criminal contempt is a class A misdemeanor carrying up to 1 year and $25,000.`,
    body: `<h2>The three-month cap on Alaska jury service</h2><p>Instead of a one day or one trial rule (the official text we read does not set one for Alaska), the state limits the total burden. Under § 09.20.025 a person may not be required to serve more than three months as a juror in any consecutive two-year period. There is one exception that matters for planning: if you are sitting on a trial when the three months run out, you must finish that trial. Add the one-year disqualification in § 09.20.020, and someone who has served recently can point to either rule when a new summons arrives.</p><h2>Deferring Alaska jury duty and the hardship test</h2><p>Section 09.20.035 allows deferral for hardship, temporary transportation problems or election work, but only to a date you agree to, and never more than 10 months after the original start date. That makes it a reschedule, not a release. A full exemption under § 09.20.030 is aimed at longer problems: your health, the care your family needs, a permanent physical or mental disability, or other hardship expected to last more than two years. Judicial officers, and teachers at a designated low-performing school during the school term, are also listed. Claim the exemption with the court and show the reason; because wages are not guaranteed by law, ask your employer in writing about its own jury leave policy.</p>`,
  },
  AZ: {
    intro: `Estimate Arizona's $12 daily juror fee plus the lengthy trial fund, see what your employer can and cannot do, and check an excuse under A.R.S. § 21-202.`,
    resume: `In Arizona the county pays each juror $12 for each day of attendance at superior or justice court (A.R.S. § 21-221), plus round-trip mileage at the state employee rate; a juror excused on the first day gets mileage only. Superior court trial jurors who lose earnings can also apply to the Fair Jury Improvement fund for $40 to $300 a day from day 1, when money is available (§ 21-222). Employers do not have to pay wages, but under § 21-236 they cannot penalize you, cannot force you to use vacation or sick leave, and must restore your position and seniority. A written request from anyone aged 75 or older must be granted. Service follows one day or one trial, and someone selected for a jury is not required to serve again for two years. You may postpone twice on request. Wilfully skipping service can bring a fine up to $500 and a new date, and a body attachment after a second summons (§ 21-223).`,
    body: `<h2>Arizona's lengthy trial fund on top of $12 a day</h2><p>The $12 county fee is low, which is why the Fair Jury Improvement fund matters. Under A.R.S. § 21-222 a superior court trial juror who loses earnings may ask the fund for between $40 and $300 a day, counted from the first day, as long as the fund has money. The fee and the fund work together: the calculator shows the county fee, and the fund can replace part of lost wages. Keep pay stubs or tax records ready, because the court will want proof of what you are losing. Mileage is paid round trip at the state employee rate under § 38-623.</p><h2>Postponements, the age 75 request and other Arizona excuses</h2><p>Arizona gives two postponements on request, whether made at the counter, over the phone, by email or on paper, to a date the jury commissioner sets; a third needs an unforeseen extreme emergency. At 75 a written request to be excused, temporarily or permanently, must be granted. Other grounds in § 21-202 include a mental or physical condition backed by a medical statement, undue or extreme physical or financial hardship, being a sole caregiver, not understanding English, temporary absence from the area or lack of transportation, and grand jury service within the last 4 years. Certified peace officers and state correctional officers are also listed. Missing work alone is not a hardship.</p>`,
  },
  AR: {
    intro: `See what we could confirm about Arkansas jury duty, mainly the job protection rule, and how to work out a juror fee once your summons gives the rate.`,
    resume: `Arkansas is the state where our reading came up shortest. The Arkansas Code is published online only through a script-based viewer, and the Arkansas Judiciary site refused connections when we checked on October 29, 2026, so the juror fee, mileage, age rule, length of service, postponement method, excuse list and penalty are all unconfirmed here. One point is documented by the Pulaski County jury administration page: under Arkansas law a person summoned for jury duty cannot be fired, lose sick leave or vacation time, or face any other penalty, provided they give their employer reasonable notice of the summons. That page does not say whether an employer must keep paying wages, so treat pay as a question for your employer's written policy. For everything else, rely on the summons itself and the clerk of the court that sent it: they state the report date, the daily rate and how to ask for a postponement or an excuse. Answer the summons by its deadline while you wait for any reply.`,
    body: `<h2>Arkansas job protection depends on notice</h2><p>The one rule we could document for Arkansas has a condition attached. According to the Pulaski County clerk's jury administration page, Arkansas law prevents an employer from firing a summoned juror, taking away sick leave or vacation time, or imposing any other penalty, but only if the employee gives reasonable notice of the summons. In practice that means telling your supervisor as soon as the summons arrives and keeping a dated copy of what you handed over. The page we read does not set a wage duty, so do not assume your employer must pay for the days you spend in court.</p><h2>What to do while Arkansas figures stay unconfirmed</h2><p>Because the state code viewer and the judiciary site could not be read, this page does not quote an Arkansas juror fee, an age exemption or a fine. When your summons states the daily rate, multiply it by your days in court to get a total before tax. If you need more time, or believe a medical condition, caregiving duty or financial hardship should excuse you, contact the court clerk named on the summons before the report date and ask what proof it needs. Respond by the deadline even if you hope to be excused; a written request on file protects you far better than silence.</p>`,
  },
  CA: {
    intro: `Find out why California pays nothing on day one, what $15 a day adds up to over a trial, and which hardship excuses rule 2.1008 accepts.`,
    resume: `California pays jurors $15 for every day starting with the second; the first day is unpaid (Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 215). Mileage of at least 34 cents a mile applies from the second day, or up to $12 a day for public transit from the first. Government employees who receive full pay from their employer get no daily fee. Private employers are not required to pay wages, but Cal. Gov. Code § 12945.8 makes it unlawful to fire or harass a juror and requires time off; tell your employer as soon as the summons arrives. Advanced age is not a ground for exemption, although a serious health problem can lead to an excuse. The state runs one day or one trial service, and after reporting or serving you are not required to return for at least 12 months. Courts should grant a one-time deferral without a court visit. Failing to appear can mean fines from up to $250 for a first violation to $1,500 for three or more, plus possible contempt (§ 209).`,
    body: `<h2>Why day one is unpaid in California</h2><p>Many Californians report once, are not sent to a courtroom and go home. Under the one day or one trial system that single day completes their service, and § 215 pays nothing for it, apart from up to $12 for public transit if they took it. The $15 fee and the 34 cent mileage start only on the second day, which means a juror on a five-day trial earns $60 in fees. If your employer has its own jury leave policy, that is usually what keeps your income whole, since the law protects your job but not your paycheck.</p><h2>How California judges a hardship excuse</h2><p>Rule 2.1008 of the California Rules of Court lists what counts as undue hardship: no reasonably available means of public or private transportation, travel over about 1.5 hours, an extreme financial burden, an undue risk to property, a physical or mental impairment, or caring for a dependent who cannot be replaced. The request goes in writing on the summons response form, with facts showing the hardship and why a deferral would not solve it; a doctor's note may be required. Courts are told to offer a deferral first, so if the problem is only timing, choose a later date online or in writing and give the earliest date you can serve.</p>`,
  },
  CO: {
    intro: `Model Colorado's split juror pay, where your employer covers the first three days and the state pays $50 from day four, and check an excuse.`,
    resume: `Colorado splits the cost of jury duty between employers and the state. For the first three days a regularly employed juror is paid regular wages by the employer, up to $50 a day (C.R.S. § 13-71-126); this includes part-time or temporary workers employed for three months or more. Self-employed jurors may ask the court for up to $50 a day over those three days under § 13-71-127, and unemployed jurors may claim certain expenses. From the fourth day, the state pays every juror $50 a day. An employer cannot fire you or withhold benefits over jury service. There is no age exemption beyond the minimum age of 18. Service is one day, or one trial if you are impaneled, and anyone who served on a jury in any court within the past 12 months is not qualified to serve. You have the right to one postponement, generally within 6 months. The penalty statute, C.R.S. § 18-8-612, could not be read on an official site, so no fine is stated here.`,
    body: `<h2>Colorado's first three days: wages, not a court fee</h2><p>During days one to three the court itself pays nothing to employed jurors. The obligation sits with the employer: regular wages, capped at $50 a day, for anyone regularly employed, a group that § 13-71-126 extends to part-time and temporary staff with at least three months on the job. If you earn less than $50 a day you receive your usual wage; if you earn more, the employer owes $50, unless its own policy is more generous. Self-employed people apply to the court for up to $50 a day, and unemployed jurors can seek reimbursement of certain expenses. From day four the state takes over at $50 for everyone.</p><h2>Postponing and the breastfeeding rule in Colorado</h2><p>The one postponement you are entitled to can be requested on the paper summons by mail or email, through your county's online postponement form, or by contacting the jury commissioner; the new date is generally within 6 months, and out-of-state students may push it up to 12 months. Students and military members are not excused automatically but can postpone. Section 13-71-119.5 covers breastfeeding, with a medical statement. Other grounds include undue or extreme physical hardship to you or someone under your care, being primary caregiver of a permanently disabled household member who does not work outside the home, and a disability that prevents service.</p>`,
    days: 5,
  },
  CT: {
    intro: `Check how Connecticut's five employer-paid days give way to a $50 state fee, see the age 75 opt-out, and test an excuse under § 51-217.`,
    resume: `Connecticut puts the first five days of jury service on employers. Under Conn. Gen. Stat. §§ 51-247 and 51-247a, a full-time employee (30 or more hours a week) must receive regular wages for those days, whatever the size of the business, and cannot be fired or coerced for serving. Part-time and unemployed jurors are instead reimbursed out-of-pocket expenses of $20 to $50 a day, which includes mileage at 20 cents a mile round trip. Starting on day six, the state covers $50 a day for everyone. People 75 or older may choose not to serve (§ 51-217(a)(8)). The term is one day, or the length of the trial if you are impaneled, and after appearing you are excused for 3 jury years (each running September 1 to August 31). Deferral to a date within 10 months can be done online. A no-show faces a civil penalty whose amount the Chief Court Administrator sets, which the court may waive; § 51-237 provides no jail term.`,
    body: `<h2>Connecticut's sixth-day switch from wages to $50</h2><p>Most Connecticut trials never reach day six, so the employer wage rule is what matters for most jurors. Regular wages are owed for the first five days, or part of them, to every full-time employee, and the statute sets no minimum company size. Day six is when the source of pay changes: the state then pays $50 for each day of service, and your employer's legal duty to pay ends, though its own policy may continue it. Part-time and unemployed jurors do not get wages from anyone during the first five days, so the reimbursement of $20 to $50 a day for out-of-pocket costs is their only money until the state fee starts.</p><h2>Deferring or asking to be excused in Connecticut</h2><p>The Judicial Branch lets you defer online to any date within 10 months of the original summons. A second deferral, or one requested within two weeks of the date, means calling Jury Administration at 1-800-842-8175. Excusal requests go through the eResponse portal, by email or by phone, and § 51-217 lists the grounds: extreme hardship, a physical or mental disability with a health care provider letter, childcare hardship, active-duty military service, not speaking and understanding English, service in a state or federal court within 3 jury years, or living outside Connecticut. Students are expected to defer to a school break.</p>`,
    days: 7,
  },
  DE: {
    intro: `Learn how Delaware's $20 daily allowance works in each county, why New Castle jurors get nothing on day one, and which excuse groups § 4511 recognizes.`,
    resume: `Delaware pays jurors a $20 per diem under 10 Del. C. § 4514, meant to cover travel, parking and other out-of-pocket costs, so there is no separate mileage. How often you receive it depends on the county. New Castle County uses one day or one trial service and pays nothing for the first day, while Kent and Sussex Counties summon jurors for a two-week term and pay the $20 for every day they report. No state law makes a private employer keep paying wages, and the employer may not count the allowance as pay; § 4515 protects your job. People over 70 may ask to be excused by returning the questionnaire with a copy of a driver's license. Superior Court and federal court together will not call you more than once every two years. A one-time date change can be made in the juror portal, though not within 5 days of service. Failing to appear and show cause is criminal contempt, punishable by up to $100, 3 days in jail, or both (§ 4516).`,
    body: `<h2>Three counties, two Delaware jury systems</h2><p>Where you live in Delaware changes what jury duty looks like. In New Castle County a juror who is not picked, or who sits on a trial lasting only one day, receives no reimbursement at all, because one day or one trial jurors are not paid for the first day. In Kent and Sussex the summons covers a two-week term, and the $20 allowance is paid for each day you are required to report. Either way the money is an expense allowance rather than wages, and the statute says your employer must not treat it as pay, which means it cannot be subtracted from a salary your employer chooses to continue.</p><h2>Delaware groups that count as undue hardship</h2><p>Delaware lists personal and occupational groups whose membership itself shows undue hardship, extreme inconvenience or public necessity: jury service within the past two years, age over 70, deeply held religious beliefs, primary caregivers of people unable to care for themselves, full-time police officers and firefighters, active full-time military or Delaware National Guard, active full-time clergy, and the self-employed or those paid mainly on commission. A breastfeeding mother is excused for at least one year. Send the qualification questionnaire back to the county Jury Services office with a written request and proof such as a doctor's note or employer letter. Residents temporarily out of state for work or study are deferred until they return.</p>`,
  },
  DC: {
    intro: `Estimate the $57 daily fee paid by DC Superior Court, see how the five-day employer rule and the small-business exemption apply, and check excuses.`,
    resume: `The District of Columbia pays $57 a day to jurors whose job does not pay them, a figure set by the Board of Judges above the $30 written in D.C. Code § 15-718. Jurors who keep receiving their salary get a $7 daily travel stipend instead, and there is no mileage. Petit jurors who are summoned but never serve on a jury do not receive the fee. For service of 5 days or less, full-time employees working in DC are entitled to their usual pay minus the juror fee, but employers with 10 or fewer employees are exempt (§ 15-718(c), § 11-1913). Anyone 70 or older may ask to be excused under the Superior Court jury plan. Petit jurors serve one day unless picked for a trial; grand jurors serve 25 workdays. Service is limited to once per 24 months. One postponement for any reason is available through eJuror, email, phone or live chat. A no-show without good cause risks up to $300 and 7 days in jail (§ 11-1907(b)).`,
    body: `<h2>The DC employer rule: five days, minus the fee</h2><p>DC is unusual in tying the employer's duty to the juror fee. A full-time employee working in the District who serves 5 days or less is entitled to usual compensation less the fee received, so the employer tops up rather than paying twice. Firms with 10 or fewer employees are not required to pay at all. Because a salaried juror receives only the $7 travel stipend from the court, the deduction is small in practice for those still on payroll. Beyond five days, or at a small employer, the $57 court fee becomes the main income, which is why the calculator starts from that rate.</p><h2>Postponing or being excused from DC jury service</h2><p>Superior Court allows one deferral for any reason, with no explanation needed. You can request it through eJuror, by email to jurorhelp@dcsc.gov, by phone at (202) 879-4604 or by live chat. Excuses cover people who are not District residents, are under 18, have a serious illness or disability, are caring for a child under 2.5 years, are 70 or older, have served in the last 2 years, or would face extreme hardship. Petit service often ends the same day: once voir dire is done and every panel is filled, jurors not chosen are finished. Grand jury service, at 25 workdays, is the longer commitment.</p>`,
  },
  US: {
    intro: `For anyone summoned to a United States district court, this page totals the $50 federal attendance fee and explains employer, excuse and deferral rules under the Jury Act.`,
    resume: `Federal jury service is governed by the Jury Selection and Service Act, 28 U.S.C. §§ 1861-1878. Jurors receive an attendance fee of $50 a day from the first day (§ 1871), plus a per-mile allowance for the shortest route; a trial juror on a case lasting more than 10 days may get up to $10 more a day, $60, if the judge orders it. Federal government employees keep their salary instead. Federal law leaves wages to the employer, but § 1875 forbids firing, intimidating or coercing a permanent employee for serving, with civil penalties up to $5,000 per violation. The statute has no automatic age exemption; most of the 94 district courts let people over 70 ask for a permanent excuse. A petit juror hears one case at most, and nobody must attend more than 30 days as a petit juror in two years. Ignoring a summons without good cause can bring up to $1,000, 3 days in jail, community service, or a combination (§ 1866(g)).`,
    body: `<h2>How the federal fee grows on a long trial</h2><p>The $50 daily fee is the same in every district, but § 1871 adds a step for long cases. A petit juror required to attend more than ten days hearing one case may receive up to $10 more than the attendance fee, at the trial judge's discretion, which brings the day rate to $60 from day 11. Grand jurors can receive the extra amount after 45 days. Travel is paid on top, at the per-mile rate the Administrative Office sets for court staff driving private cars, with tolls covered and parking at the court's discretion. The calculator opens at 12 days so the step is visible.</p><h2>Each district court writes its own federal jury plan</h2><p>Many details live in the local jury selection plan rather than the statute. Term length, the one day or one trial practice and group excuses all vary among the 94 district courts. Most plans let three groups ask to be excused: people over 70, anyone who served on a federal jury within the past two years, and volunteer firefighters, rescue squad or ambulance crew members. Active-duty military, professional fire and police officers and full-time public officers are barred from service rather than excused. To ask for a deferral or an excuse for undue hardship or extreme inconvenience, use the eJuror page of the court that summoned you and pick an alternate time if the deferral is granted.</p>`,
    days: 12,
  },
  FL: {
    intro: `See how Florida's juror pay jumps from $15 to $30 on day four, who gets nothing for the first three days, and which § 40.013 excuses apply.`,
    resume: `Florida's juror fee, set by Fla. Stat. § 40.24, has two tiers. For the first three days the clerk pays $15 a day, but only to jurors who are not regularly employed or whose wages stop; employees who keep their pay receive nothing from the clerk for those days. From day four every juror receives $30 a day. Florida employers owe no wages for jury days, though § 40.271 protects your job. Anyone 70 or older is excused on request and can ask in writing to be excused permanently (§ 40.013(8)). Florida runs one day or one trial service statewide, and someone who reported as a prospective juror in their county within the past year is exempt for one year. The clerk can postpone service up to 6 months on a written or oral request, or up to 12 months during a declared emergency, and you may propose a date. Failing to attend without sufficient excuse carries a fine up to $100 and may also be treated as contempt (§ 40.23(3)).`,
    body: `<h2>Florida's day-four pay increase in practice</h2><p>The two-tier fee rewards long service and protects people who lose income. Picture two jurors on a five-day trial. One is unemployed: she receives $15 for each of days one to three and $30 for days four and five, a total of $105. The other keeps his salary from his employer: the clerk pays him nothing for the first three days and $30 for each of the next two, so $60 in fees on top of his wages. If your employer stops paying you, tell the clerk, because that is what makes you eligible for the early $15 days. The calculator opens at five days so the change on day four is visible.</p><h2>Florida excuses for parents, students and caregivers</h2><p>Section 40.013 is detailed about family situations. An expectant mother may be excused, as may a parent not employed full time who has custody of a child under 6, and anyone who gave birth in the 6 months before the reporting date. Caregivers of a person incapable of caring for themselves, people with a permanent physical or mental incapacity backed by a physician statement, and full-time students aged 18 to 21 are covered too. Full-time law enforcement officers are listed, while practicing attorneys, physicians and the physically infirm are excused at the judge's discretion. Make the request to the clerk of court in the county that summoned you.</p>`,
    days: 5,
  },
  GA: {
    intro: `Model a Georgia juror allowance at the rate your county sets, between $5 and $50 a day, and check the age 70 and caregiver exemptions before you respond.`,
    resume: `Georgia leaves juror pay to its counties. Under O.C.G.A. § 15-12-7 each county sets a daily expense allowance between $5 and $50, and most pay around $25; the $5 used by the calculator is only the statewide minimum, so enter your county's figure. The allowance is paid from the first day, including jury selection. Employers must give time off for jury duty under O.C.G.A. § 34-1-3, but that time may be unpaid. People 70 or older may claim an exemption and can sign an affidavit to be removed from the jury list permanently (§ 15-12-1.1(b)); contact the court before the appearance date. The length of service is also local: if you are not selected you are released, though some counties summon jurors for a week. Anyone who served on a jury in the last 12 months is not eligible. Deferrals are handled by the county clerk of superior court. Failing to respond may lead to a fine or contempt, but no statewide amount appeared on an official page we read.`,
    body: `<h2>Georgia's county-by-county jury allowance</h2><p>Because each county picks its own daily allowance within the $5 to $50 range, two Georgians on the same five-day schedule could receive $25 or $250 depending on where they were summoned. The state's own guidance says most counties pay about $25. Your summons or the clerk of superior court in your county will state the exact rate. With no wage duty for employers, that allowance may be your only pay for the days in court, so ask your employer early whether its policy covers jury leave. No official site displayed the Georgia Code text itself, so the figures here come from the state's official jury duty page and the Fayette County clerk.</p><h2>Claiming a Georgia exemption by affidavit</h2><p>Georgia exemptions are claimed, not automatic. Fayette County, for example, uses the Affidavit for Exemption printed on the back of the summons, which must be notarized and sent with documentation. The grounds in O.C.G.A. §§ 15-12-1 and 15-12-1.1 include age 70 or older, full-time students, active military members and their spouses, the primary teacher in a home study program, the primary caregiver of a child age 6 or younger, the primary unpaid caregiver of a person unable to care for themselves, a permanent disability backed by a doctor's affidavit, and not living in the county. The same county grants a one-time deferral, with documentation, through that affidavit.</p>`,
  },
  HI: {
    intro: `Calculate Hawaii's new $50 daily juror fee and 33 cent mileage, then check the exemptions and excuses, from age 80 to living more than 70 miles away.`,
    resume: `Hawaii raised its juror fee from $30 to $50 a day on July 1, 2026, the first increase since 1989, and adds round-trip mileage of 33 cents a mile from home to court. Jurors excused at their own request may receive mileage only. No Hawaii law requires an employer to keep paying wages, although some do so voluntarily or under union contracts; Haw. Rev. Stat. § 612-25 bars firing an employee because of jury duty. People 80 or older may claim an optional exemption on the juror questionnaire. Selection usually takes one day, and a juror who is chosen serves for the whole trial and deliberations, often three to four days. Only one deferral is allowed per summons, requested by writing or calling the court (in writing in the 2nd, 3rd and 5th circuits). The official text we read sets no rule on how soon you can be summoned again and no penalty for missing service, because the state statute site refused access when we checked on October 29, 2026.`,
    body: `<h2>Hawaii's 2026 juror fee increase</h2><p>For more than three decades a Hawaii juror received $30 a day. Since July 1, 2026 the rate is $50, so a four-day trial now pays $200 in fees before mileage instead of $120. The mileage rate of 33 cents a mile applies to the round trip from home to court, which matters on islands where the courthouse can be a long drive away. If you ask to be excused and the court agrees, you may still receive mileage for the trip, but not the daily fee. Because employers are not required to pay wages, check whether your union contract or company policy covers jury leave.</p><h2>Hawaii grounds for exemption or excuse</h2><p>The Judiciary's list of grounds is broad: age 80 or older, elected officials during the legislative session, judges, practicing physicians and dentists, active military deployed out of state, police, fire and emergency medical personnel, people living more than 70 miles from the court, mothers breastfeeding a child aged two or under, serious personal hardship, caregiving with no substitute, and a disability or medical condition backed by a physician certificate. Some of these are optional exemptions, such as age 80, which you simply claim on the juror questionnaire. Any other excuse request must be written, signed and documented, then mailed, emailed or hand delivered to the court on the summons at least two weeks before the appearance date.</p>`,
    days: 4,
  },
  ID: {
    intro: `Work out an Idaho juror payment from the $10 full-day floor or your county's higher rate, and see how county practice shapes terms, excuses and deferrals.`,
    resume: `Idaho sets only a floor for juror pay: at least $5 per half day or $10 per full day, plus mileage from home to the courthouse at the county employee rate. Counties may pay more, and Bingham County, for example, pays $25 per half day and $50 per full day, so the $10 in the calculator is a minimum to replace with your county's rate. Employers may not discharge, threaten or penalize an employee because of jury service, but paying wages is left to the employer. Jurors aged 70 and up can request release by marking the jury qualification form. Much of the rest is county practice: in Bingham County a panel term lasts four months, and after a full term you may ask not to serve again for two years. Postponement follows the instructions on your summons. The Idaho statutes were out of reach on October 29, 2026 (the legislature's site refused connections), so the code sections, any one day or one trial rule and the penalty are unconfirmed here.`,
    body: `<h2>Idaho's half-day juror fee and county rates</h2><p>Idaho counts jury attendance in half days as well as full days. The statewide minimum is $5 for a half day and $10 for a full day, and many counties go well beyond it. In Bingham County a full day pays $50 and a half day $25, five times the floor. Mileage is added at the rate the county pays its own employees for driving, measured from your home to the courthouse. Since employers are not required to keep paying wages, the county rate is what decides how much a long panel term costs you. Check the summons or the jury commissioner for the figure used in your county.</p><h2>Four-month panels and postponement in Idaho</h2><p>Some Idaho courts summon jurors to a panel for a term rather than for a single day. Bingham County's term is four months, and once you have served a full term there, a request not to serve again within two years will be granted. Idaho has no automatic exemptions: requests go to the county jury commissioner with documentation. The grounds named by the Idaho Supreme Court and Bingham County include age 70 or older, a temporary or chronic medical condition with a physician's letter, active military duty, being a nursing mother, a religious obligation and childcare problems. Some of these lead to a move to a later panel rather than a full excuse, so read the instructions sent with your summons.</p>`,
  },
  IL: {
    intro: `Read what applies to Illinois jurors, from the Cook County $35 daily fee to the statewide ban on penalizing employees, and check your excuse options.`,
    resume: `Illinois pays jurors through its counties, and the statewide fee statute (55 ILCS 5/4-11001) could not be read because the General Assembly site was unreachable on October 29, 2026, so no statewide minimum is confirmed here. In Cook County the Circuit Court pays $35 for each day served. Under 705 ILCS 305/4.1 an employer may not deny time off for jury duty or discharge, threaten, intimidate or coerce an employee for serving, but it does not have to pay wages. A statewide age exemption was not verified; Cook County runs a Senior Citizen Opt-Out Program that lets people 70 or older call before their date to decline or transfer. Cook County also follows a one day or one trial system and does not call jurors again for 12 months; practice in other counties was not verified. Nursing mothers are excused on request by statute (705 ILCS 305/10.3). Not appearing can lead to contempt and a fine or other penalty, with no statutory maximum confirmed.`,
    body: `<h2>Cook County jury rules most Illinois jurors meet</h2><p>Because Cook County is where many Illinois jurors are summoned, its rules fill most of the gaps left by the unreachable statute site. The court pays $35 a day, so a three-day stint comes to $105 before tax. If the date on your summons does not work, call (312) 603-JURY: the date can be pushed back 11 or 22 weeks, for reasons such as a vacation, being a student or teacher during the school term, or seasonal work. People 70 or older can use the senior opt-out by phone before their date, either declining or transferring their service. Jurors summoned in other counties should follow their own summons, since rates and terms there were not verified.</p><h2>Documented excuses for Illinois jurors</h2><p>Cook County asks for documentation for each excuse. The grounds it lists include a medical condition, active military service, a pending lawsuit in the county, living in a nursing home or institution, extreme financial hardship, having no childcare, and being the primary caretaker of another person. A nursing mother stands on firmer ground: 705 ILCS 305/10.3 excuses her on request. Whatever the reason, ask before the report date rather than simply staying home, because the court treats a missed appearance as a serious matter that can end in contempt. Your employer cannot refuse the time off, and it cannot punish you for serving.</p>`,
  },
  IN: {
    intro: `Model Indiana's stepped juror pay, $30 a day before a jury is seated and up to $90 once a trial runs long, and check the age 75 exemption.`,
    resume: `Indiana pays jurors in three steps under Ind. Code § 33-37-10-1. In circuit and superior courts a juror receives $30 for each day of attendance until the jury is impaneled, then $80 a day through the fifth day of trial, and $90 a day from the sixth trial day; counties may add a supplemental fee, and city and town courts pay $15 a day. Mileage is paid at the state employee rate, or a county may pay parking instead. Employers have no duty to continue wages, but they cannot take adverse action against you or require you to use annual, vacation or sick leave, provided you notify them within a reasonable period after the summons arrives. People 75 or older may be exempted by notifying the jury administrator (§ 33-28-5-18(d)). You serve until the end of the first trial you are sworn for, or until selection is complete if not chosen. One deferral for hardship is available as of right. Failing to appear or complete service is criminal contempt, with no set maximum.`,
    body: `<h2>How Indiana's three pay steps work</h2><p>Indiana counts its higher rates in trial days, not in days since you first reported. The $30 rate covers each day you attend before a jury is impaneled, which for most people is the selection day. Once sworn, you move to $80 a day through the fifth day of trial, and the sixth trial day brings $90. Anyone not selected receives the $30 rate and is done when selection is complete. Counties may add a supplemental fee on top of these amounts, and mileage at the state employee rate is paid separately, unless your county pays parking instead. The calculator opens at seven days so all three steps appear; on a real case, count the trial days from the day you were sworn.</p><h2>Breastfeeding, small employers and deferral in Indiana</h2><p>Indiana lets a woman who has given birth in the preceding 365 days and is currently breastfeeding be exempted if she wishes. A physical or mental disability can also justify an exemption, though a physician certificate may be required, and the facts are recorded under oath. One provision protects small businesses: when an employer with 10 or fewer employees already has a worker on jury service, a second employee can be rescheduled. For other conflicts, the right to one deferral for hardship, extreme inconvenience or necessity lets you contact the jury administrator by phone, email, letter or in person and pick a new date within one year. Lying on the qualification form is a Class C misdemeanor.</p>`,
    days: 7,
  },
  IA: {
    intro: `See how Iowa juror pay rises from $30 to $50 after the first week, what the two-year rule means, and how to postpone up to 12 months.`,
    resume: `Iowa Code § 607A.8 sets juror pay at $30 a day and lets the Iowa Supreme Court add more after seven days; the Judicial Branch now pays $50 a day from day 8. Round-trip mileage at the Supreme Court's rate and actual parking costs are added. No Iowa law requires employers to keep paying wages, though § 607A.45 protects your job; the courts advise giving your employer notice the moment you are summoned. There is no age-based exemption, so older jurors need a medical, disability or other good-cause reason. Each county court sets the term, up to 3 months. If you served as a state grand or trial juror in the past two years, you are excused. Postponement up to 12 months can be requested online or by phone at least 10 days before the reporting date, and the first request is granted automatically. Failing to appear without sufficient cause can be punished as contempt: before a district judge, up to $500, six months in jail, or both (§§ 607A.36, 665.4).`,
    body: `<h2>Iowa's raise after the seventh day</h2><p>The statute fixes the base at $30 and leaves the long-service rate to the Supreme Court, which has set $50 a day from the eighth day of service. A juror who attends nine days therefore receives seven days at $30 and two at $50, or $310 before mileage and parking, and the calculator opens at nine days to show the change. Parking is reimbursed at the actual cost rather than a flat amount, so keep your receipts. With no employer wage duty, a juror on a trial of a week or more depends on these figures and on the employer's own leave policy.</p><h2>Iowa caregiver and breastfeeding excuses have a work condition</h2><p>Two Iowa excuses apply only to people who do not work outside the home. A sole caregiver of a person with a permanent disability living at home qualifies, and so does a breastfeeding mother caring for her child, but either excuse falls away if you are employed outside the home. Other grounds under §§ 607A.5 and 607A.6 are a physical or mental disability backed by a doctor's note and state jury service within the past two years. Hardship, inconvenience or public necessity usually leads to a deferral instead. Send written proof with the questionnaire, and remember that the automatic first postponement must be requested at least 10 days ahead.</p>`,
    days: 9,
  },
  KS: {
    intro: `Calculate a Kansas juror fee at your county's rate, between $10 and $50 a day, and see why Kansas fines unexcused absences day by day.`,
    resume: `Kansas leaves the daily juror fee to each county commission, within a range of $10 to $50 set by K.S.A. 43-171; the $10 figure is only the statutory minimum, so replace it with your county's rate. Mileage is paid at the rate authorized by law for travel to and from court. No Kansas statute requires a private employer to keep paying wages: K.S.A. 43-173 only bars firing a permanent employee for jury service. The law lists no age-based exemption, so an older person can be excused only for physical or mental infirmity or hardship. Length of service is set by each district court; Sedgwick County, for instance, calls panels for one week, or until the trial ends if you are selected. Anyone who served as a juror in the same county within the preceding year is excused. To reschedule, call the county jury clerk before the summons date. An unexcused absence can cost up to $100 for each day missed, and contempt is also possible (K.S.A. 43-165).`,
    body: `<h2>The per-day fine in Kansas</h2><p>Most states cap the fine for skipping jury duty at a single amount. Kansas counts it by the day: K.S.A. 43-165 allows a fine of up to $100 for each day of unexcused absence, so missing a week-long panel could add up quickly, and the court may also hold you in contempt. The fix is simple and lawful. If you cannot attend, call the jury clerk before the date on your summons and explain why, with documents. In Sedgwick County one rescheduling is allowed for a temporary extraordinary or compelling hardship.</p><h2>Who Kansas courts excuse</h2><p>K.S.A. 43-158 and 43-159 list the grounds a court will consider: a physical or mental infirmity, presence elsewhere that the public welfare, health or safety requires, an extraordinary or compelling personal hardship, service as a juror in the county within the past year, and not being able to understand English. A felony conviction within 10 years also stands in the way of service. Breastfeeding leads to a postponement rather than a full excuse. Send supporting documents, such as a doctor's note, with your request to the jury clerk. Because neither wages nor an age exemption are guaranteed in Kansas, ask early, and check whether your employer offers paid jury leave.</p>`,
  },
  KY: {
    intro: `Find the $12.50 Kentucky juror payment broken into its two parts, the 30-day service cap, and the excuse and postponement routes under KRS 29A.`,
    resume: `A Kentucky juror receives $12.50 for each day of attendance, made up of $5 for service and $7.50 as expense reimbursement (KRS 29A.170), and it is paid even on days you are not seated. No statute requires an employer to keep paying wages, but KRS 29A.160 forbids firing, threatening or coercing an employee over jury service, and a violation is a Class B misdemeanor under KRS 29A.990. People 70 or older may ask on the juror qualification form to be excused for the period summoned (KRS 29A.080(2)(h)). Nobody can be required to serve more than 30 court days on a petit jury in a 24-month period, unless needed to finish a case, and the same period limits you to one grand jury and not both grand and petit service. Postponements are handled by the Chief Circuit Judge or a designated clerk or administrator. A juror who fails to appear is ordered to show cause and may be punished for contempt; KRS 29A.150 sets no specific amount.`,
    body: `<h2>How Kentucky postpones instead of excusing</h2><p>Kentucky judges favor postponement or reduced service over a permanent excuse, and the statute gives them room to do it. Staff designated by the Chief Circuit Judge can push your service back up to 12 months, while the judge can postpone it as far as 24 months. Ask before, or on, the day you are summoned. A permanent exemption is reserved for a permanent medical condition, and other excuses under KRS 29A.100 are undue hardship, extreme inconvenience and public necessity. Breastfeeding or expressing breastmilk is also listed, along with age 70 or older on request.</p><h2>The 30 court day cap for Kentucky jurors</h2><p>Rather than a one day or one trial rule, which nothing we read for Kentucky provides, Kentucky limits the total. Within any 24 months you cannot be required to serve more than 30 court days as a petit juror, except to complete a case already under way, and you cannot be put on more than one grand jury or on both a grand and a petit jury. Pay stays the same throughout at $12.50 a day, so a full 30-day stretch would bring $375 before tax. Since that is modest and employers need not pay wages, ask your employer for its jury leave policy as soon as the summons arrives.</p>`,
  },
  LA: {
    intro: `Check the $25 Louisiana juror fee for criminal cases, the one paid day your employer owes, and the age 70 exemption in the state constitution.`,
    resume: `Louisiana's statutory pay for jurors in criminal cases is $25 a day, paid by the parish under La. R.S. 13:3049(B)(1)(b), plus mileage at the state officials' rate for one trip each way to the courthouse; pay for civil juries is set separately by the court and can differ. Under La. R.S. 23:965 an employer must grant up to one day of jury leave without loss of wages, sick, emergency or personal leave, provided you notify it within a reasonable time after the summons; beyond that day pay is not required. People 70 or older are exempt under the state constitution (art. V, § 33) and may decline or choose to serve. The official text we read sets no statewide term or one day or one trial rule. The 21st Judicial District says anyone who served in the last two years may claim an exemption and allows one postponement within six months. A district judge can fine a no-show up to $50, jail them up to 3 days, or both.`,
    body: `<h2>Louisiana's one day of paid jury leave</h2><p>Louisiana sits between states with no wage rule and states that require several paid days. Section 23:965 obliges every employer to give up to one day of leave for jury duty with no loss of wages or benefits, and it cannot be charged to sick, emergency or personal leave. To keep that protection, tell your employer within a reasonable time after you receive the summons and before you appear. From the second day, the employer may stop paying, and the parish fee of $25 a day becomes your court income. An employer who fires someone for jury service faces a fine of $100 to $1,000 for each employee affected.</p><h2>Postponing and proving hardship in a Louisiana parish</h2><p>Parishes set their own procedure, so call the clerk of court before your date. In the 21st Judicial District you may postpone once, to a date within six months, if you have a valid reason. Excuse requests go to the judge with documents such as tax returns, doctor's statements or proof of dependency, and they must be made before your appearance date. A mental or physical condition with physician documentation can lead to an excuse for up to 24 months; undue or extreme physical or financial hardship can cover caring for a dependent, daily living costs or illness. Lost time at work does not count as hardship on its own.</p>`,
  },
  ME: {
    intro: `Total Maine's $15 daily juror fee and mileage, see the five-year and three-times-in-a-lifetime limits, and check the age 80 excuse before you call the clerk.`,
    resume: `Maine pays $15 for each day a juror is required to attend, plus mileage from home to court and back at the state rate set in 5 M.R.S. § 8 (14 M.R.S. § 1215). No statute makes an employer keep paying wages, but 14 M.R.S. § 1218 forbids firing, threatening or cutting health insurance because of jury service; that is a Class E crime, and lost wages can be recovered for up to 6 weeks. Anyone 80 or older who does not wish to serve is excused on request (§ 1213(2)(C)). The usual call is 15 court days, longer only to finish a case, although most jurors in practice serve one day of selection. Trial jury service is limited to once in any 5-year period and three times in a lifetime. To postpone, contact the court clerk before your date; the court must approve and may move you to a less burdensome season. Contempt after a show-cause hearing can bring a fine up to $100, up to 3 days in jail, or both (§ 1217).`,
    body: `<h2>Maine's lifetime limit on jury service</h2><p>Few rules are as generous to repeat jurors as Maine's. Once you have served, you cannot be required to serve again for five years, and nobody can be made to sit as a trial juror more than three times in a lifetime. If a new summons arrives inside that window, tell the clerk and include proof of the earlier service. The standard call covers 15 court days, but most people summoned spend only a single day in selection, which at $15 plus mileage rarely replaces a day's pay. The employer protections in § 1218 go further than many states, since cutting your health insurance because of jury duty is also forbidden.</p><h2>Asking a Maine court to excuse or postpone you</h2><p>Grounds listed in 14 M.R.S. §§ 1211 and 1213 include undue hardship, extreme inconvenience, public necessity, a physical or mental disability (a physician certificate may be required), age 80 or older, active military service, work as an election official during elections, and service within the past 5 years. Breastfeeding leads to a postponement. Write to the clerk with documentation, such as a medical note for a health reason. Self-employment alone is not accepted as an excuse, but the court can move your service to a less burdensome season, which may suit someone whose work peaks at a particular time of year.</p>`,
  },
  MD: {
    intro: `Work out Maryland juror pay, $30 a day plus any county supplement and $50 from day six of a trial, and see the evening shift protection.`,
    resume: `Maryland pays a state per diem of $30 a day under Md. Code, Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 8-426 and 8-427, and counties may add a supplement, for a total generally between $30 and $40; a trial juror receives $50 a day from the sixth day of one trial. Wages are not owed by employers, but §§ 8-501 and 8-502 bar them from making you use annual, sick or vacation leave, and from requiring a shift that starts after 5 p.m. (or before 3 a.m. the next day) once you have served four hours or more. People 70 or older are exempt if they ask the jury commissioner in writing (§ 8-306). Each circuit court's jury plan sets the length of service. You need not serve more than once in three years unless a county plan says otherwise. Participating counties offer a one-time online date change. Failing to appear can bring up to $1,000 and 60 days in jail (§ 8-504).`,
    body: `<h2>Maryland's limit on work after a jury day</h2><p>Maryland is one of the few states that regulates what your employer can ask of you on the same day. Under § 8-502, if you spend four hours or more in jury service, the employer cannot require you to work a shift that begins after 5 p.m. that day or before 3 a.m. the next morning. Together with the rule against forcing you to use annual, sick or vacation leave, that keeps a juror from working a full shift around court. Pay is a separate matter: no wage duty exists, so the $30 state per diem, any county supplement and the $50 rate from the sixth day of a trial may be all you receive.</p><h2>Maryland excuses, exemptions and how long they last</h2><p>Exemptions under § 8-306 cover people 70 or older who ask in writing and active military members. The other grounds are extreme inconvenience, public necessity and undue hardship, and they also cover a breastfeeding mother and the sole caregiver of a child under 3. Show the reason on the questionnaire, in an interview or with other evidence. An excuse lasts only as long as the reason does, and a person is limited to two unless the circumstances are extraordinary. For a simple date conflict, participating counties let you change the report date once online; elsewhere, contact the local jury office with a pressing reason.</p>`,
    days: 7,
  },
  MA: {
    intro: `See how Massachusetts splits juror pay, three employer-paid days and then $50 a day from the state, and check the age 70 opt-out and postponement rules.`,
    resume: `Massachusetts makes every employer pay regular wages for the first three days of jury service, including part-time, temporary and casual staff (M.G.L. c. 234A §§ 48, 49). From day four the state pays $50 a day, with no extra travel reimbursement, and unemployed, retired and other non-employed jurors can claim up to $50 a day of expenses for days one to three. A court can excuse an employer facing extreme financial hardship, in which case the state pays up to $50 a day instead. People 70 or older can opt out by saying so on the summons response (§ 4(2)). Trial service is one day or one trial statewide, while grand jurors serve three months, and anyone who served on a state or federal jury in the previous three years is disqualified. One postponement of up to a year is available as of right on the summons response. Failing to appear is a crime punishable by a fine of up to $2,000, and the court may issue an arrest warrant (§ 42).`,
    body: `<h2>Massachusetts employers pay the first three days</h2><p>Unlike most states, Massachusetts does not leave the first days of jury duty to chance. Section 48 requires every employer to pay regular wages for days one to three, and it covers part-time, temporary and casual employees as well as full-time staff. Only a court can relieve an employer, and only for extreme financial hardship; the state then pays the juror up to $50 a day. Because of the one day or one trial system, many jurors finish within those three days. Those who sit longer move to the state's $50 daily fee from day four, which is where the calculator's five-day default shows the switch.</p><h2>Getting out of or moving Massachusetts jury service</h2><p>The summons response is where most requests happen. You can choose your one postponement there, to any date within a year, and claim a disqualification with the required letters: a physician letter for a physical or mental disability, or for being the sole daily caregiver of a permanently disabled household member. Age 70 or older, service in the last three years, moving out of the judicial district and not being able to speak and understand English are also grounds. A full excuse from trial service needs a court finding of extreme hardship, which courts decide strictly. Further deferrals beyond the first are only for hardship, inconvenience or public necessity.</p>`,
    days: 5,
  },
  MI: {
    intro: `Estimate Michigan juror pay from the $30 first-day and $45 later-day minimums, see the half-day rates, and check the age 70 and nursing mother exemptions.`,
    resume: `Michigan sets minimum juror fees in MCL 600.1344: since April 1, 2018, at least $30 for the first full day ($15 for a half day) and at least $45 for each later full day ($22.50 for a half day), plus mileage of at least 20 cents a mile. Counties may pay more with county board approval, so these are statewide minimums. Employers must release you for jury duty and cannot discharge or discipline you for it (MCL 600.1348), but they are not required to keep paying wages. People over 70 may claim an exemption by asking for it (MCL 600.1307a), as may nursing mothers. Each court sets its own term: state law allows one day or one trial service but does not require it. Service as a juror in the previous 12 months disqualifies you. Postponement is handled by each court's jury clerk. Missing court when summoned is contempt, capped at $7,500 and 93 days in jail (MCL 600.1346, 600.1715).`,
    body: `<h2>Michigan's half-day juror rates</h2><p>Michigan pays by the half day as well as the full day, which matters because many jurors are released at lunchtime. A half day on the first day is worth at least $15, and later half days at least $22.50; full days are $30 for the first and $45 after that. A county can raise these minimums with its board's approval, so your summons or jury clerk will give the exact figure. An employer that disciplines or fires a juror can be found in contempt and charged with a misdemeanor. Many employers continue pay voluntarily, though the law does not require it.</p><h2>Terms, postponements and exemptions across Michigan courts</h2><p>Because each court runs its own jury system, the length of service varies. MCL 600.1371 lets courts adopt one day or one trial service without making it mandatory, and the state best-practice manual recommends no more than two weeks. Postponements are handled by the jury clerk; the 15th District Court, for example, grants only one, so contact the clerk as soon as a conflict appears. Exemptions for age over 70 and for nursing mothers must be requested and the status proven. Other excusals, such as for a medical condition with a physician statement, are at the court's discretion, and job reasons are generally not accepted.</p>`,
  },
  MN: {
    intro: `Calculate Minnesota's $20 daily juror fee with 54 cent mileage, see how county size sets your term, and check the automatic age 70 excuse.`,
    resume: `Minnesota pays jurors $20 for each day they must report, plus 54 cents a mile for the round trip from home to the courthouse; the rates are set statewide by the Judicial Branch under the authority of Minn. Stat. § 593.48. Child care and parking can be reimbursed, with parking deducted from the daily rate. Employers have no wage obligation, but under § 593.50 they must release you and cannot force you onto another shift on reporting days. Anyone 70 or older who asks is excused automatically, with no proof needed (Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 808(e)). The term depends on county population, from two weeks in the largest counties to up to four months in the smallest, and service ends after 10 days actually reported. You are not qualified again for four years after state or federal service, or two years if your county runs short of jurors. Failing to appear without good cause is a misdemeanor: up to $1,000, 90 days in jail, or both.`,
    body: `<h2>How county size sets a Minnesota jury term</h2><p>Minnesota ties the length of your term to the population of the county that summons you. Counties of 100,000 or more use a two-week term. Those between 50,000 and 100,000 can run terms of up to two months, and counties of 50,000 or less up to four months. Whatever the term, your service ends after you have actually reported on 10 days, so a long term in a small county does not mean months of daily attendance. The $20 fee is paid only for the days you must report, which keeps the total modest even over a long term.</p><h2>Minnesota's four-year gap and its approach to excuses</h2><p>Minnesota gives one of the longest breaks after service: four years before you can be qualified again, counting state and federal juries, cut to two years only when a county lacks enough prospective jurors. The court rules have abolished automatic excuses except those in Rule 808. Beyond the age 70 request, you can be excused for a continuing hardship to yourself or the public, a disability with medical documentation if required, or an impaired ability to receive and evaluate information, which a judge decides. Legislators and their staff during session, and candidates for elected office, are deferred rather than excused. Ask the jury commissioner, who will usually prefer a deferral.</p>`,
  },
  MS: {
    intro: `Model a Mississippi juror fee at your county's rate, from $25 to $40 a day, and see the over-65 exemption and the single postponement right.`,
    resume: `In Mississippi each county board of supervisors sets the daily juror fee for circuit, chancery and county courts, between $25 and $40 under Miss. Code § 25-7-61; justice courts pay $10 to $15. Mileage is paid at the rate in § 25-3-41, and a Lengthy Trial Fund can top up wages in civil trials lasting more than 10 days. Employers have no statutory wage duty, but under § 13-5-35 they cannot take adverse action or push you to spend annual, vacation or sick leave, as long as you tell them about the summons within a reasonable time. Anyone over 65 is exempt on request (§ 13-5-25); so is anyone with jury service in the previous two years. The official text we read sets no term length or one day or one trial rule. One postponement, to a date within six months, is a right. A no-show without good cause faces up to $500, 3 days in jail, or both, and possible community service (§ 13-5-34).`,
    body: `<h2>Mississippi's Lengthy Trial Fund and the fee cap</h2><p>The county fee range has not moved recently: bills to raise the cap died in the 2024 and 2025 sessions. For long civil cases, the state offers a second source of money. When a civil trial runs more than 10 days, the Lengthy Trial Fund can top up the wages a juror loses, which helps people whose employer does not continue their pay. The figures here come from the legislature's reproductions of the bills, because the code itself is hosted by a private publisher. Ask the circuit clerk which daily fee your county has adopted, since it can be anywhere from $25 to $40.</p><h2>Postponing or being excused in Mississippi</h2><p>You have a right to postpone your first appearance once, as long as you have not postponed in the past two years. Reach the circuit clerk (at the office, by telephone, by email or by letter) and fix a new date within six months or two court terms; a second postponement needs an unforeseeable extreme emergency. Excuses are split. An illness that makes you incapable of serving can go straight to the clerk with a physician certificate, while hardship claims, such as having no substitute caregiver or costs that threaten daily living expenses, go to a judge with documents like tax returns and must be ruled on before the reporting date. Members of the Mississippi National Guard on active duty can be excused with a statement from a superior officer.</p>`,
  },
};
